-- title:
--   Matching node residues at pairs of places from local node rings
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $N\ge 1$, a perfect field $k$ of characteristic $q$ with a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red}\colon A\to k$, modular polynomial data `data` at $q$ satisfying the Kronecker congruence `hKr`, integrality hypotheses $h\alpha,h\beta$ for the two level-raising maps $N\mid Nq$, a place specialisation $P$ of this collection and a prolongation tuple $R$ over $P$ (two prolongations $R_1,R_2$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}(X_0(Nq))=$ `modularFunctionFieldBar (N * q)` with residue maps to `modularFunctionFieldC k N` $=k(j,j_N)$). Let $W$ be a finite set of places of `modularFunctionFieldC k N`, and write $\varphi$ for `arithFrobC q k N`, the semilinear automorphism induced by the Frobenius of $k$. Assume `hmax`: for each $w\in W$ and each number field $K\subset\overline{\mathbb Q}$ there is a larger number field $K'$, node coordinates $c=(x,y)$ of $R$ at $w$ over $K'$ and an element $\varpi$ of the coefficient subring $A\cap K'$, such that the node ring $R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\,K'\,w$ is local and Noetherian, $\varpi$ generates the kernel of `redRestrict red K'`, the maximal ideal of the node ring is generated by the constant $\varpi$ together with $x$ and $y$, every element of the node ring differs from some constant by a non-unit, and every element has first node residue zero or of non-negative order at $w$ and second node residue zero or of non-negative order at $\varphi\cdot w$. Then for every $f\in\overline{\mathbb Q}(X_0(Nq))$ lying in the integers of both $R_1$ and $R_2$, and every pair $s$ in the image of $W$ under `smulNodePairEmb` for $\varphi$, if $f$ has non-negative order at every place $V$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}(X_0(Nq))$ with $P.\mathrm{reduceFst}\,V=s_1$, then there is a single $c\in k$ such that $s_1$ takes the value $c$ at the first residue of $f$ and $s_2$ takes the value $c$ at the second residue of $f$ (taking the value $c$ meaning the element lies in the valuation subring and its residue is the image of $c$).
--
--   This is the node clause of the regularity law: at the pairs of places attached to $W$ (the supersingular points in the intended application) the two residues of a function integral for both prolongations agree, reflecting the normal crossing of the two components of the special fibre of $X_0(Nq)$ at $q$. It is used in the construction of a common unit with a pole, via `exists_commonUnit_pole_of_reduceFst_fixed_ordinary_residueField`.
